WEBVTT

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:02.370
Kirupa Chinnathambi: 
Alright.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:03.210
Welcome, everyone.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.730
Welcome. Great to see you all
in person, as well as to those

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.920
of you watching online.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.360
So, here's the thing.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.360
Agents today, they're no
longer confined to a checkbox.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.450
They're not waiting for you
and I to tell them what to do.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.620
They're taking action.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.820
They're calling tools.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.860
They're running
workflows end-to-end

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:20.760
that are pretty complex

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.400
that would have been almost
unimaginable just a few

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.330
months ago.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.340
So, the problem is this.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.110
It's not about
capabilities anymore.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.240
Agents are plenty capable

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.790
and we're a lot more productive
as a result of that.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.650
No, what we want
is we want agents

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.080
to run safely and predictably.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.970
And in this session,
we're going to learn all

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.700
about the capabilities
Windows is providing

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.710
to help your agents
do just that.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:44.970
My name is Kirupa.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.020
I'm a Product Manager

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.700
in the Windows Platform
and Developer team.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.340
I'm joined by Stuart,
an architect also

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.250
on the Windows Platform and
Developer team, and Patrick,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.020
a Project Manager at GitHub.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.790
You'll be hearing
plenty from Stuart

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.310
and Patrick throughout
this session.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:02.400
Now, before we go into
the technical details,

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.780
I want to take a step back.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.250
As you can see from
these headlines,

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.370
the way agents are being
used today; it's not great.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.550
As agents are gaining more
capabilities and work

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.220
with greater autonomy, they're
causing real problems.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.320
Either in the wild,
or maybe even

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.660
within our own organizations.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.150
And if I had to
generalize, the reason is

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:25.680
that we're moving very
quickly from AI that responds

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.160
and answers to AI that
acts with agency.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.380
So, when an agent
makes a small mistake

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.740
like sending an unwanted
email or modifying a file,

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.340
or even making something public
it shouldn't, it gets amplified.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.870
It creates a chain reaction.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.200
The blast radius for the damage
it causes is much larger.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.840
Here's a great example
where, using an agent,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.510
it's doing some coding, deletes
your production database.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:49.650
Not great.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.380
Even situations where you
might have seen this example

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.910
in your news feeds
a short while ago,

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:57.750
where the agent is operating on
your email, they're telling you

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.360
to stop, don't delete it, agent
acknowledges, goes ahead

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.860
and deletes it anyway.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.050
These are the kinds of
examples that we need

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.180
to close at a platform layer.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.390
Because when agents can operate
on things that matter to us,

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:13.430
files, code, email, all the
systems you and I rely on,

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:16.250
we cannot rely on the
agent to self-govern.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.420
We cannot rely on the agent

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.970
to basically follow
good intentions.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.590
Even best-effort prompting

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.020
and prompt engineering
can't solve this either.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.570
It really requires a
more holistic solution.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.990
And also, because it's not
just the agent itself.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.060
The entire environment
the agent operates

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.030
in is a potential risk vector.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.880
An agent can be talking to
humans, and there's risk.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.340
Agent to tools, agent
to applications,

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.500
agent to agent, or
even agent to LLM.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.910
All of these are risk vectors,

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:46.710
and each of them have
the risk profile

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:49.230
that we cannot possibly
account for all

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.630
from just our own vantage point.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.180
And this is not just a
theoretical problem.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.180
So, I'm going to move away
from examples and headlines

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.520
to something that teams
are actively exploring.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.710
I'm going to turn it over to
Patrick to talk about what he

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.770
and the GitHub Copilot
Team have identified.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:05.520
Patrick Niloletich: 
Wonderful.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.500
Thanks, Kirupa.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.080
At GitHub, we get the
unique opportunity to see

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.860
where modern model performance
intersects with user behavior,

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.410
and how those trends
alter over time.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.450
You take a look at
the Copilot CLI.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.240
One of the most common
occurrences that happens

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.270
over time is users
start auto-approving,

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.160
removing the gates and
letting their agents run

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.260
for longer and longer sessions.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.530
When you scale it up to the
Copilot SDK, it rides on top

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.330
of our Copilot CLI server.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.040
But one of the unique
differences that we did

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.920
when we built the Copilot
SDK was design it in a way

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.110
that was made for
SDK integrators,

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:42.540
and not for Copilot CLI users.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.610
What that led to was a mismatch

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:46.380
between our permissions
model inside

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:48.660
of our SDK, and one in our CLI.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:52.110
Certain integrators expected our
SDK to be fully configurable,

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.480
come with nothing out
of the box already set.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.400
All the CLI users expected it

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.740
to protect their current
working directory,

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.320
and other artifacts
on their system.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.850
When you look a
little bit farther

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.040
into the orchestration side,

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.960
our orchestrator
inside the Copilot CLI

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.850
and SDK has become
much more powerful,

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.740
especially over the
last six months,

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:14.280
where a job typically ran from
3 to 5, 10 minutes or so,

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.320
they can now run for
30 minutes or more,

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.790
which means that the problems

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.520
in these two first segments
compound exponentially

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:24.250
with time, especially as agents
get more and more powerful.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.180
Kirupa Chinnathambi:
Thank you, Patrick.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.340
So, the problem is very clear.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:32.220
Agents are operating with high
agency, and they're running

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.110
with limited to no guardrails.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:37.020
Then the question then shifts
to, what does the platform need

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.350
to provide to make
agents safe by design?

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.910
That's where Windows comes in.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.910
From its very beginning,
Windows has always evolved

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.880
to handle the needs 
of its users.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:48.800
The part that's changed is this.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.810
The definition of a
user is changing.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.070
What is a user?

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:55.550
Today, they now include agents
acting on their behalf

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:58.670
and that detail fundamentally
changes how Windows

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:01.230
and the platform need to react.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:03.890
And to account for this,

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:06.290
there are three key
platform capabilities

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:09.110
that we're introducing that when
agents adopt, that can work

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:11.000
with agents as a
new type of user,

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.240
and also ensuring agents can
run safely and reliably.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.730
Let's walk through very quickly
what these perimeters are,

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:19.140
and we'll go into more
detail in a few moments.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.390
First it's agent identity.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:24.030
The agent needs to have
an identity, distinct

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.010
from that of the human user.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.930
Second, is containment.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:30.330
We don't want our agent
to have full access

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:34.800
to all the capabilities
that we have as people.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.390
We can't give it
unfettered access

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:38.790
to all the files and
all of our data.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.100
What we need is, we need
to have agents running

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:42.420
in a controlled environment

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:44.820
where their blast
radius is limited.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:47.370
And the last one
is manageability.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:51.420
For many of us, that means an
IT admin can deploy, monitor,

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.610
govern, and enforce the policies

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:57.510
that dictate what an agent can
do, and also what it cannot do.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:00.480
So, to dive deeper into this,
starting with identity,

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.190
I'm going to turn
it over to Stuart.

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:02.460
Stuart Schaefer: 
Great.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.230
Thank you, Kirupa.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.270
So, as Kirupa said, one of
the most interesting things

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:09.510
about agents is, what is
the identify of an agent?

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:13.110
I'm here to tell you,
agents aren't you.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:16.620
No matter what we do today, the
funny thing about where we got

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.980
to is, most agents
run as you the user.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.980
If you use a CLI, they are
running in your session.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.690
They have access
to all your data.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:27.240
The program itself is
controlling what they do.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.620
But they're not you.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.260
Imagine what would happen if
the agent could get access

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:35.010
to almost anything from you
and do whatever it wants.

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:38.910
We need the system to be
able to enforce a separation

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:41.820
between an agent and
you, and to be able

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:44.760
to enforce the
capabilities and promises

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.800
that define what an agent is,
different than what a user is.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.400
In general, what a user is and
what they do on a machine today,

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.380
can have a lot more
rights than some

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.840
of what we want our
agents to be able to do.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.450
Do you want your agent to
be admin on your machine?

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:02.370
It's a question you should ask.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.740
So, for us, the way we
think about it is this.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:08.100
Agents are a new type of
identity in the system.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:11.880
When you think about users
and devices and applications,

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.070
we identify those on the system.

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:18.510
Agents aren't just the processes
of the CLI that you're using,

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:21.090
or whatever agentic
application that you're using.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:24.630
They themselves are an
intrinsic first-class entity

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:25.620
on the system.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.630
Just like users,
apps, and devices,

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.270
they need to have a
security principle.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:32.880
We need to be able to make
assertions about them.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:36.150
Things that define
security permissions

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:38.640
that the system
itself can enforce.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.920
We need to be able
to author policies

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:43.350
that define what are the
things that they are allowed

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:46.710
to be members of, to
join, to understand

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:50.280
in general what an agent is
allowed to do in the system.

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:54.390
And whatever they do needs
to be audited by the system.

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:57.540
We need to have logs that
show what an agent does

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:00.240
so that we can separate
the actions of the agent

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.550
from the actions of
the user itself.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.520
So, with that, we're going to
do a quick demonstration here.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:06.150
And we'll show you.

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:09.330
We have worked with the
OpenClaw team to work

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:13.860
on showing how an agent like
OpenClaw, an agentic system,

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.260
can function in an
operating environment

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:20.560
where it is separated
from you, the user.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:26.400
In this demo, what we'll see
is, this is a Teams chat.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.000
And in Kirupa's Teams chat,
he will go in and talk

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.670
to his OpenClaw gateway node.

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:36.540
The OpenClaw gateway node is
running in a separate session

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:40.440
on the same machine, but as
a different user account

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.510
and in a different
session, which means all

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:46.070
of its processes are
separated from the desktop,

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:49.010
from the Windows, and from all
the processes that are here.

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.230
And by default,
everything that it tries

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:55.170
to accomplish has no access to
what Kirupa himself is doing.

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:58.800
So, as he interacts with the
agent, it is fully separated,

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.890
fully identified by
Windows at its core.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:04.950
So, that is how we look
at agent identity.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:09.310
It is not you, and it should
run separated from you.

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.180
So, after agent identity,
the next thing

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:19.890
that Kirupa introduced
was containment.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.770
A really important
thing about agents is,

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:25.650
is that when agents
operate, as Kirupa said,

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.680
they need a blast radius, the
things that they can accomplish.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:30.720
What's really
important is for us

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.350
to understand, agents
are non-deterministic.

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.690
Different than the software
that you run today,

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:39.810
when we install an
application, at compile time,

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:43.350
what that software can
do is already defined.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:46.200
Our agents today write
their own software.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:49.980
They evolve; they change
on a very frequent basis.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:54.690
And as they operate with agency,
they make decisions themselves.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:58.860
Not your mouse clicks, but the
decision of the software itself.

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:01.650
That non-determinism is
something that we need to think

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:04.230
about how that happens
in the system.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:07.230
When an agent can choose
its own actions at runtime,

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.940
that makes it unpredictable.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.250
But because it's
unpredictable, it doesn't mean

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:12.960
that it's uncontrollable.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.800
It means that what it needs is a
boundary around what it can do.

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:19.740
A boundary that's
understandable.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:23.670
That we can understand
what it can and cannot do.

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:28.230
That isolation boundary
allows us to control risk

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:31.740
and to understand, what
is the profile of risk

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:35.100
that that agent can have
against our system.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:38.670
Also, what's really interesting
is, once you put an agent inside

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:42.570
one of these sandboxes, it
allows us to enforce the design

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:45.930
of least privilege, which
is, we can take the actions

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:49.530
of an agent, its individual
actions, and put it inside

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:52.500
of a sandbox, and
let that one sandbox,

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:54.570
or that one action, have policy

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:56.640
that defines just
what it can do.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:59.160
And then when the agent
decides to do something else,

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:02.130
we'll build another
sandbox for that.

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:04.770
What that means is is that the
sandboxes that we're going

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:06.840
to build need to scale.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:10.680
The isolation boundary needs to
be proportionate to the risk,

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.110
and proportionate to the
action that's happening.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:16.800
For that, we've introduced what
we call the Microsoft Execution

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:18.390
Containers library.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:21.330
The MXC library is a
library that's designed

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:25.890
to help you create
containers, based on policy.

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:29.250
The developer and the agent
can declare the security

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:32.850
requirements of their agent
and the agent's actions.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:36.750
The library will then compile
that together and maps

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:41.100
that against the policies of the
system, and the policies defined

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:44.970
by IT, governed by tools
that we're used to using

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:48.150
to deploy things, to manage 
them, and monitor them.

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:52.140
Once that match has occurred,
the MXC library will then look

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:54.960
at the native OS
platform and try

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:59.160
to assemble together the
right container for the job.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:03.510
That process is something we
call dynamic composition.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.880
That means that
when an agent runs,

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:08.520
there isn't a fixed container.

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:13.740
The MXC library allows you to
create the container on the fly.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:17.430
It will, as I said, take the
definition of the application

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:19.950
that you want to run,
or the actual action.

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:23.250
It will take the user's
consent and permissions.

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:27.420
It will take the policy
defined by IT and try to from

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:31.020
that, craft together a
configuration of a container.

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:33.870
With that configuration,
it will then look

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.090
at the operating
system primitives

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:38.880
that are available
for it to use.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:40.590
It's not just Windows.

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:45.630
It's Windows, WSL, Linux, a
full cross-platform library

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:51.450
that can scale everything from
tiny, small processes to VMs,

00:12:51.450 --> 00:12:54.510
micro VMs, firecracker-style
micro VMs,

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:57.030
all the way up to
cloud-based PCs.

00:12:57.030 --> 00:12:58.680
So, MXC really allows us

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.410
to define a security
boundary that's designed

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:05.610
and proportionate to the task
that we want to accomplish.

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:08.190
So, what does that really
mean for us as developers?

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:09.510
How do we use this?

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:12.390
Well, let's dive in
a little bit deeper.

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:15.060
MXC, as I said, is a
declarative approach.

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:17.010
What it's really
designed to say is,

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:20.010
let's look at the guardrails
we want to put in place

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:21.810
for then components
of our agent.

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:24.060
What is the composition
of the container?

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:25.800
What are the security
requirements,

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:28.530
and what does the
workload shape look like?

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:33.600
So, as we dig into a little
detail, this is a Python script.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:37.830
That Python script needs to be
able to access my certificates.

00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:41.100
Certificates to authenticate
somewhere in the system.

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:44.670
It needs to be able to run
within a certain timeframe.

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:49.020
It can only access read/write
locations very much specified

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:49.500
by here.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:52.590
Everything else is
denied by default.

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:54.420
What network am
I allowed to talk to?

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:57.750
I can only talk to the
GitHub API server.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:01.320
And even more restrictive,
I can't touch the UI

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:04.710
of anything else in the system,
but I can see the clipboard

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:06.810
and read from it if
something has done that.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:08.310
That allows me to specify

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:12.030
with very fine-grained detail
what the container is,

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:15.150
and what the action
of the agent can do.

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:17.250
So, let's do a quick demo.

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:18.720
I'm going to escape out of here,

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:20.670
and do something
really dangerous

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:23.400
and bring up a command prompt.

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:24.510
So, the first thing I'm going

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:28.420
to show you, here, is a
really simple demo.

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:48.180
There we go.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:48.840
Fantastic.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:50.070
Awesome. Thank you.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:53.430
Alright. So, here is
my command prompt.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:54.540
So, from the command prompt,

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:57.750
what I'll show you here
quickly is, this is essentially

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:00.000
that script here.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.590
I built, for here, a script that
will create a PowerPoint file.

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:08.790
That PowerPoint slide will be
generated using a Python script.

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:13.980
That Python script will launch
and create the PowerPoint file

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:15.600
in this build demo folder.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:21.220
So, if I come over here, and
I will tell this to run.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.900
What you'll see here is
-- oh, I have a mistake.

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:26.610
It says, I can't do it.

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:29.730
The agent has run that script
inside of a container.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:31.200
That container has failed.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:32.400
Why has it failed?

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:36.210
Because it said, access to the
build demo folder is read only.

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:37.080
Let's come in here.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.190
We'll change that quickly.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:46.680
And we'll run it again.

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:48.360
And what you see here is,

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:51.520
this is a PowerPoint file
generated by my script.

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:54.230
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:55.550
So, that's a simple demo.

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:58.220
But the reality of it is,
what are we really here for?

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:00.990
We're here to contain an agent

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:04.740
that may have written
scripts that aren't perfect.

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:09.120
So, what I did here
is, I created a demo

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:15.900
that shows an agent running
and a monitor of the agent.

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:19.470
This agent is purposefully
malicious, and will try

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:21.330
to escape its boundaries.

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:23.700
The first thing it's going to
do is try to see if it can get

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:27.270
around the file system right
to the user's SAM hive,

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:29.250
see if it can steal
into user.net,

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:31.470
see if it can grab the
user's credentials.

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:32.610
What we'll see here is this.

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:35.490
On the right-hand side, those
red things are it firing

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:38.580
and hitting the boundaries
of the sandbox itself.

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:41.310
So, listening to events
from the sandbox,

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:44.700
we can tell that the sandbox is
enforcing the constraints here.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:48.060
So, this is in live,
this running demo,

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.520
pounding against the sandbox,
us listening to that.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:52.200
What can a developer
do with that?

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:52.980
Two things.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:56.160
One, you can monitor and observe
what actually is happening

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:57.240
in the agent.

00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:02.040
Two, if you want to know, why
did my agent fail, you can pull

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:05.670
that data back in for the next
loop around, and understand how

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:08.880
to evolve and make better
policy for your agent.

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:11.070
So, there's that.

00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:13.290
The last thing I want to
show you quickly is this.

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:17.670
As I said before, we have
many different sizes

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:19.440
and shapes of containers.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:23.910
The smallest one is based on a
technology we call Hyperlight.

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:28.980
Hyperlight is a small VMM that
will start here a container,

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:32.100
which is a hardware-backed
virtual machine.

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:35.130
The hardware-backed virtual
machine runs a tiny operating

00:17:35.130 --> 00:17:39.540
system called Nanvix that is
about 10 megabytes in size.

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:46.740
It will start up and run that
same PowerPoint file very fast.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:51.060
Boom. And if we look, that
is the same exact script

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:54.630
of the other side that generated
that same PowerPoint file,

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:58.290
loaded incredibly fast in a
very small about of memory,

00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:02.220
and fully protected
by the hypervisor.

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:08.160
So. With that, my demo
has actually succeeded,

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:08.790
which is fantastic.

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:09.020
Exactly

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:12.900
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:16.710
So, given all that,
as Kirupa said,

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:20.490
all those things put together
are useful ingredients,

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:24.850
but the agent itself still
needs to be managed.

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:29.490
The platform that we operate
in needs to make sure

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:32.550
that the management system
that we have can guarantee

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:35.910
that the agents are operating
within defined policies.

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:40.080
So, IT or you the user, need to
be able to specify what you do

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:42.480
and don't allow
your agents to do.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:43.740
The policies need to be able

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:46.440
to control what can
the agent access?

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:48.240
What are the actions
they are allowed to do?

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:51.720
And as we showed, how do
we observe those actions

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.270
to make sure that
they are correct?

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:57.660
In general, we also believe that
one of the most important things

00:18:57.660 --> 00:18:59.610
for you, as a
developer, is to think

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:02.220
about the concept we
call supervision,

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:06.120
which is that agents need to
operate with clear oversight.

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:09.150
The user needs to understand
whether it is a human

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:12.900
in-the-loop agent, or a
fully autonomous agent,

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:15.870
they need to be able to
trust what it can do.

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:19.020
The most important thing is
that as an agent becomes more

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:22.200
and more autonomous, that
trust needs to be something

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:25.560
that we build upon to make
sure the user believes

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:29.310
and IT believes they still
have operational control.

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:31.354
We think you
need to think about,

00:19:31.366 --> 00:19:33.180
how do you surface the intent

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:35.310
of your agent at
natural way points.

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:38.610
Make sure that even though
an agent can do things fully

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:41.220
autonomously, it
should still ensure

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:44.570
that it's notifying the user,
providing places for it

00:19:44.570 --> 00:19:46.520
to compensate if
there are problems.

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:49.040
Why? Because agents
make mistakes.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:51.350
Assume that your
agent is not perfect,

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:53.460
and that you've designed
it in such a way

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:56.490
that your agent can compensate
and bring the human user

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:58.590
into the loop to
compensate for it.

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:02.700
And make sure that you think of
consent as a continuous thing.

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:05.580
Not just as Patrick said,
one time you set it

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:07.290
up this way and it's perfect.

00:20:07.290 --> 00:20:09.900
Agents will evolve
policy and change,

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:11.550
and need to keep
asking the user.

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:15.180
Why? Because we earn
autonomy with evidence.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.610
The more that we can build
confidence in our users

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:21.030
to trust the things that we do,
that's where we'll build more

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:25.100
and stronger and richer
autonomous systems.

00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:27.020
With that, we
believe in Windows,

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:29.150
there's a lot of features
that are in the platform

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:32.250
and that we can add that
help you use the surfaces

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:33.030
that are there.

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:36.180
The activity that your agent
is running under can surface

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:37.800
that using Windows channels.

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:39.480
It can use notifications.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:41.340
It can use many things
that exist there,

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:44.040
as well as the guardrails
and safety primitives

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:45.620
that we help you show today.

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:49.520
Further, if an agent has its
own identity, the providence

00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:51.500
of its code, and an audit

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:56.130
of what it does is a natural
part of what the OS does.

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:58.440
So, with that, I'll turn
it over to Patrick.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:20:59.890
Patrick Nikoletich: 
Wonderful.

00:21:01.290 --> 00:21:05.460
In the Copilot CLI and SDK, we
started all the way over there.

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:07.290
No sandboxes, no controls.

00:21:07.290 --> 00:21:09.540
Basic guardrails in
the application.

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:12.120
If the user hit the wrong prompt
at the wrong point in time,

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:13.200
they ended up in a land

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:16.670
where they could do a lot more
damage than they expected.

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:19.550
Today, we're happy to announce
that the Copilot CLI and SDK,

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:21.230
through our collaboration
with the team

00:21:21.230 --> 00:21:24.650
over at Windows, were able to
ship a sandbox out-of-box,

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:27.590
which is currently under
the experimental flag.

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:29.210
We'll be moving to
GA in the future,

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:31.950
so it will become a 
default on solution.

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:34.530
In the future, we're moving
to a fully isolated state

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:37.410
where you'll take the entire
agentic host, shove it inside

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:40.260
of a safe environment, and
allow it to execute freely,

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:44.430
enabling you to accomplish a
wider range of jobs end-to-end

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:46.740
without supervising
the agent directly,

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:50.220
or worrying about what it's
doing and where it's going.

00:21:50.220 --> 00:21:51.960
To look at this in a
more practical sense,

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:54.270
let's break down a little
bit of what Stuart showed

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:57.270
into three short little demos.

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:59.490
Inside of the GitHub Copilot
CLI, like I mentioned,

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:01.620
you can actually
fire it open today.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:04.320
And if you've enabled
the experimental flag,

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:07.930
you will be able to -- let me
make sure it's actually running.

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:14.880
Perfect. You will be able

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:18.330
to access a /sandbox 
slash command ,

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:20.880
which not only will enable
you to turn it on and off,

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:22.320
but as you'll see,
you'll have some quick,

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:23.520
handy, out-of-the-box options

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:25.350
that allow you to
configure policy.

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:27.060
You can turn on and
off the sandbox.

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:29.490
You can set the file
system configuration.

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:31.410
Notice "include working
directory" is already there,

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:33.990
and the policy will
clear after it's done.

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:36.810
If we just enable the
file system protections,

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:39.300
and we ask the agent to just go
off and delete all the files

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:41.160
on our desktop, we
probably don't really want

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:42.060
that to happen.

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:44.880
But it's a good test
for the agent itself.

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:46.710
We experience our normal
permissions prompt,

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:47.430
and we said, yes,

00:22:47.430 --> 00:22:48.990
go delete everything
on my desktop,

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:50.880
which, definitely don't
want that to occur.

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:52.200
I have plenty there.

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:53.910
And you'll notice
that the agents,

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:56.880
because it has the sandbox
enabled, it's able to 1,

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:59.760
block the deletion of all
the files on the desktop.

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:01.050
Protection number 1.

00:23:01.050 --> 00:23:02.850
Two, the agent's
able to identify

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:05.160
that it's actually operating
with the sandbox on,

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:07.920
so you're aware of what's
providing that protection.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:10.590
And then 3, there are
some options there,

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:12.750
and some next-level guidance
for what you can do next

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:16.770
to move your way around the
sandbox if you need to.

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:21.180
If we look at the next
piece -- go forward one for me.

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:22.230
Perfect.

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:24.930
We go forward to restricting
outbound network access.

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:26.430
You have a similar opportunity.

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:29.220
Just offer the file system and
towards the network ingress

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:30.990
and egress to your system.

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:33.960
You look at the Network tab, you
can allow outbound connections.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:35.340
You can allow local network.

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:36.540
They're separate, so
you have a couple

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:38.340
of different flex options there.

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:41.250
But the beautiful thing
is, if you ask the agent

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:42.960
to do something as
sketchy and dangerous

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:45.060
as turn your private
repository public,

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:46.920
which might be company IP,

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:50.490
the agent can actually use those
filters to block certain actions

00:23:50.490 --> 00:23:52.710
and prevent you from making
dangerous calls outside

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:53.670
the system.

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:56.910
And while this might seem like
a basic demo of me asking it

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:58.920
to do something and
expecting some guidance,

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:02.460
the thing to be aware of is.
agents are still exposed

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:04.680
to prompt injection
attacks in the modern era,

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:06.150
which means that any context

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:09.240
that enters its world sphere can
actually direct its guidance

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:11.580
and force it to accomplish
or take care of actions

00:24:11.580 --> 00:24:14.230
that you wouldn't
necessarily expect it to.

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:21.390
If we scale this up to
the CLI and SDK, perfect,

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:23.970
you have an opportunity to
leverage all these capabilities

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:26.940
in any application that you
integrate or harness into.

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:29.460
And so, this is a personal
writing app that I use to hack

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:32.010
on the Copilot SDK itself.

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:33.960
I'll go ahead and I'll
run a sandbox text.

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:35.310
The way that I
actually deploy agents

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:37.770
out of here is, I just write
like I normally would in a file,

00:24:37.770 --> 00:24:41.310
and I can just @mention an
agent, and it will deploy it.

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:44.340
Because I'm actually
using my @sandbox agents,

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:47.580
which comes with these
policies enabled by default,

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:50.520
what I'm able to do is,
similar to the first demo,

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:52.980
ask it to delete
my desktop files.

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:54.720
The sandbox will
block the action.

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:56.820
You will see the calls
failing in there.

00:24:56.820 --> 00:24:59.820
More importantly, you will see
the agent providing its final

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:02.640
guidance, saying the
sandbox blocked my action.

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:03.930
It's prevented me
from doing this.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:06.030
You need to find
another way around.

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:08.370
Now, in a normal situation,
I wouldn't want

00:25:08.370 --> 00:25:09.480
to find another way around.

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:12.750
I don't want to delete
files again on my desktop.

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:16.650
So, if we take a simpler,
easier-to-manage scenario,

00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:20.040
such as creating a
hello.txt on my desktop,

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:22.800
I can show you what it's
like to build finer

00:25:22.800 --> 00:25:26.100
and greater experiences for
customers using the SDK.

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:28.590
Again, all of these scenarios
are available, and you can think

00:25:28.590 --> 00:25:31.650
about how you want to integrate
them best into your solution.

00:25:31.650 --> 00:25:33.360
What's important about
this one is, I want it

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:34.650
to create a file.

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:36.330
The sandbox blocked it.

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:38.280
But because this is
integrated in the SDK,

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:41.190
and you can not only detect
when the sandbox is in action,

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:44.070
but you have an opportunity to
perform other actions on behalf

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:47.430
of the user, you can simply
hit "Disable the sandbox."

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:48.300
Let it continue.

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:51.570
I reviewed the command, which
allows it to operate freely.

00:25:51.570 --> 00:25:53.400
Create the file on the desktop.

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:55.650
We should see this thing
minimize in a second.

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:57.630
And you'll notice
there's a file there.

00:25:57.630 --> 00:26:00.240
Allowing you to not only
put the sandbox in place

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:02.700
as a great default
out-of-the-box option,

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:05.790
but putting user experiences and
other controls that allow users

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:08.040
to navigate safely around
it when they run into it,

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:10.140
and they expect the
agent to be successful.

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:12.840
Kirupa Chinnathambi:
Thank you, Patrick.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:15.870
So, part of us building all
these platform capabilities,

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:17.400
we actually work very
closely with Patrick

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:20.790
and the GitHub Copilot team to
design and validate the design.

00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:22.205
But we also work very closely

00:26:22.217 --> 00:26:23.940
with a broader
ecosystem as well,

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:26.460
with partners like
OpenAI, Manus,

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:28.800
Hermes, NVIDIA, and OpenClaw.

00:26:28.800 --> 00:26:31.950
Stuart gave a great demo, and in
the keynote today you saw more

00:26:31.950 --> 00:26:35.160
about how Windows primitive
are helping OpenClaw run

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:37.530
more securely.

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:40.560
So, we covered a lot of
ground in this talk.

00:26:40.560 --> 00:26:42.990
Let me go ahead and summarize
the key things that you need

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:45.990
to do to make sure agents
run well on Windows.

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:49.590
First, every agent should
be running identified.

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:51.870
It should have its own identity,
distinct from that

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:54.180
of the human user,
with its own identity,

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:56.340
permissions, and audit trail.

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:57.720
It should run contained.

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:02.160
Every action runs in the right
sandbox, enforced by policy.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:03.840
And it also runs managed.

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:05.281
Now, this is a
topic that's going

00:27:05.293 --> 00:27:06.660
to require a lot more coverage,

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:09.510
which we will cover in future
sessions, and blog posts,

00:27:09.510 --> 00:27:11.790
and videos, but what we
will do is give a very

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:13.200
high-level overview.

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:16.860
At a high-level, IT admin
policies apply consistently

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:19.890
across devices, with
full enforceability

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:21.120
and observability.

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:24.030
Now, if we go one level deeper,
here's an extra feature you get

00:27:24.030 --> 00:27:27.510
as part of using MXC
to build your agents.

00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:30.240
You get Agent 365
native integration.

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:33.330
And this enables your agents
running in Windows, too.

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:35.670
Start secure and stay secure.

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:39.030
And this also means you get
the full ecosystem as well.

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:42.390
You get Defender, Entra,
Intune, and Purview.

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:45.210
Their protections allow
your security and IT teams

00:27:45.210 --> 00:27:48.240
to constrain and
secure local agents

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:52.110
to make them run more securely
in enterprise context.

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:54.150
That's really like a nice
icing on top of this,

00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:56.490
to take it back
to where we started from.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:58.860
As agents move from
answering questions

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:00.600
and to taking real actions,

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:04.080
the biggest challenge is
ensuring they run well.

00:28:04.080 --> 00:28:07.620
Windows is the foundation
to handle that shift.

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:11.190
And Windows isn't just a
place where agents can run.

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:13.530
It's the place where
they can run reliably,

00:28:13.530 --> 00:28:16.050
safely, and at scale.

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:20.250
And with that, if you haven't
